Instructs RPM to look for Armored PGP keys on the local disk when needed.

Using Keyring Path support, we point rpm to a local directory where it can
read the key when requested. This can make it easier for external programs
to manage the keys that RPM uses to verify packages. When used with
feature/rpm-required-sig, it makes it easier to make the key available to
RPM for authorized installations.

The local key should be installed to /etc/rpm/keys/ (you may need to
create this directory). The key file is in the format of an ASCII
armored file. You can get the key using the following command:

$ gpg --list-keys

pub   2048R/5D64AD52 2015-10-14
uid                  testuser (nocomment) <testuser@testemail.com>
sub   2048R/CBE5FB13 2015-10-14

The key can be exported using:

$ gpg --export -a "testuser" > test.key

Then copy to /etc/rpm/keys on the target.

Q & A
1. How to prepare signed rpm packages and signed repository for testing?

Here is an example referencing oe-core:
1) Load the public/private key to your build system, there is a test
   pair in oe-core with the key name "testuser".

$ gpg --batch --import \
  path_to_project/layers/oe-core/meta-selftest/files/signing/key.pub \
  path_to_project/layers/oe-core/meta-selftest/files/signing/key.secret

$ gpg --list-keys
------------------------------------
pub   2048R/5D64AD52 2015-10-14
uid                  testuser (nocomment) <testuser@testemail.com>
sub   2048R/CBE5FB13 2015-10-14

2) Build a signed rpm package (e.g. "ed") and a signed repository

$ mkdir -p path_to_build_ed
$ . path_to_project/oe-init-build-env .

Add the following lines to conf/local.conf:

INHERIT += "sign_rpm"
RPM_GPG_PASSPHRASE = "test123"
RPM_GPG_NAME = "testuser"

INHERIT += "sign_package_feed"
PACKAGE_FEED_GPG_NAME = "testuser"
PACKAGE_FEED_GPG_PASSPHRASE_FILE = "path_to_project/layers/oe-core/meta-selftest/files/signing/key.passphrase"


$ bitbake ed

$ bitbake package-index

3) Copy the public key to the target

$ scp path_to_project/layers/oe-core/meta-selftest/files/signing/key.pub root@<target_ip>:/etc/rpm/keys


2. How to access remote repository via http?

1) Install and start Apache HTTP server

$ systemctl start apache2

2) Create repository

$ sudo ln -snf path_to_build_ed/tmp/deploy/rpm /var/www/html/rpm

3) Add repo to target system

$ cat >> test.repo << END
[test]
name=test_name
baseurl=http://<http-server-ipaddress>/rpm
enabled=1
END

$ scp test.repo root@<target_ip>:/etc/yum.repos.d/

#@TYPE: Wrtemplate
#@NAME: rpm-local-keys
#@DESCRIPTION: Instructs RPM to look for Armored PGP keys on the local disk when needed.
